-------------------------------------------------
Most useful tables / columns for the request
-------------------------------------------------
`bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stops`
 • stop_id : primary key that identifies each stop  
 • stop_name : to locate the two required stops

`bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stop_times`
 • trip_id : links a stop to a specific trip  
 • stop_id : foreign‑key to stops → lets us know which stop name it is  
 • stop_sequence : tells the order in which a vehicle reaches a stop within a trip  
 • arrival_time : scheduled arrival (needed for Sacramento St & Davis St)  
 • departure_time : scheduled departure (needed for Clay St & Drumm St)

`bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.trips`
 • trip_id : primary key → joins to stop_times  
 • route_id : the route to which the trip belongs (for the requested output)  
 • trip_headsign : given in the question

Extra conditions we are likely to use  
 • LOWER(stop_name) LIKE '%clay st & drumm%'  (to find the stop_id for Clay St & Drumm St)  
 • LOWER(stop_name) LIKE '%sacramento st & davis%'  (to find the stop_id for Sacramento St & Davis St)  
 • clay.stop_sequence < sacramento.stop_sequence  (to make sure Clay comes first in that direction)  

-------------------------------------------------
Exploratory → progressively‑complex SQLs
-------------------------------------------------

```sql
-- 1. Which stop names contain the word "clay"?
SELECT DISTINCT stop_id, stop_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stops`
WHERE LOWER(stop_name) LIKE '%clay%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Which stop names contain both “clay” and “drumm”?
SELECT DISTINCT stop_id, stop_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stops`
WHERE LOWER(stop_name) LIKE '%clay%' AND LOWER(stop_name) LIKE '%drumm%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Which stop names contain both “sacramento” and “davis”?
SELECT DISTINCT stop_id, stop_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stops`
WHERE LOWER(stop_name) LIKE '%sacramento%' AND LOWER(stop_name) LIKE '%davis%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Look at a few stop‑times rows for the Clay St & Drumm St stop_id(s)
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stop_times`
WHERE stop_id IN (/* put the clay stop_id(s) discovered above */ 14752, 14753)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Look at a few stop‑times rows for the Sacramento St & Davis St stop_id
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stop_times`
WHERE stop_id = /* sacramento stop_id */ 14910
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Join the Clay stop‑times to trips to see headsigns and routes
SELECT st.trip_id, t.route_id, t.trip_headsign, st.departure_time
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stop_times` AS st
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.trips`       AS t
  ON st.trip_id = t.trip_id
WHERE st.stop_id IN (14752, 14753)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Retrieve BOTH stops (Clay & Sacramento) that belong to the same trips,
--    keep their sequence numbers side‑by‑side
SELECT clay.trip_id,
       clay.stop_sequence   AS clay_seq,
       clay.departure_time  AS clay_depart,
       sac.stop_sequence    AS sac_seq,
       sac.arrival_time     AS sac_arrive
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stop_times` AS clay
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stop_times` AS sac
  ON clay.trip_id = sac.trip_id
WHERE clay.stop_id IN (14752, 14753)       -- Clay St & Drumm St
  AND sac.stop_id  = 14910                 -- Sacramento St & Davis St
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Same as #7 but keep only rows where the Clay stop comes BEFORE Sacramento
SELECT clay.trip_id,
       clay.stop_sequence AS clay_seq,
       sac.stop_sequence  AS sac_seq
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stop_times` AS clay
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stop_times` AS sac
  ON clay.trip_id = sac.trip_id
WHERE clay.stop_id IN (14752, 14753)
  AND sac.stop_id  = 14910
  AND clay.stop_sequence < sac.stop_sequence
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Earliest departure at Clay AND latest arrival at Sacramento
--    for each individual trip where Clay precedes Sacramento
SELECT clay.trip_id,
       MIN(clay.departure_time) AS earliest_clay_depart,
       MAX(sac.arrival_time)    AS latest_sac_arrive
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stop_times` AS clay
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stop_times` AS sac
  ON clay.trip_id = sac.trip_id
WHERE clay.stop_id IN (14752, 14753)
  AND sac.stop_id  = 14910
  AND clay.stop_sequence < sac.stop_sequence
GROUP BY clay.trip_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL QUERY: one line per route & headsign
--     earliest departure from Clay St & Drumm St and latest arrival at
--     Sacramento St & Davis St among trips where the direction criterion holds
SELECT
  t.route_id,
  t.trip_headsign,
  MIN(clay.departure_time) AS earliest_depart_from_clay,
  MAX(sac.arrival_time)    AS latest_arrive_at_sacramento
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stop_times` AS clay
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.stop_times` AS sac
  ON clay.trip_id = sac.trip_id
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco_transit_muni.trips`       AS t
  ON clay.trip_id = t.trip_id
WHERE clay.stop_id IN (14752, 14753)    -- Clay St & Drumm St stop_id(s)
  AND sac.stop_id  = 14910              -- Sacramento St & Davis St stop_id
  AND clay.stop_sequence < sac.stop_sequence
GROUP BY t.route_id, t.trip_headsign
ORDER BY t.route_id
LIMIT 20;
```

Run the 10th query (after confirming the correct stop_id values in queries 1‑3) to obtain the required results: for every route whose trips visit Clay St & Drumm St before Sacramento St & Davis St (i.e. a single travel direction), you will get  
 • earliest scheduled departure time from Clay St & Drumm St (HH:MM:SS)  
 • latest scheduled arrival time at Sacramento St & Davis St (HH:MM:SS)  
 • along with the trip headsign that riders see.
